@* Batch VC-1 fix round 1 (Adjudication F, controller ruling 1 -- REJECTS the
   original CompositionHost's per-host wrapper trade "as shipped," adopts the
   reviewer's third option verbatim): a ChildContent-taking wrapper. The
   HOST page's own outer keydown/keyup div (Reader's shift-tracking
   ancestor) stays exactly where it always was -- the PARENT of this
   component -- so the DOM ancestor relation the shift-tracking mechanism
   depends on (a common ancestor of .reader-page and ExplorerPopover) is
   preserved byte-for-byte; this component learns nothing about keyboards.
   See the batch report's own Adjudication F section for the full trade
   discussion this replaces.

   This component now owns EVERYTHING R3 named and Adjudication F's own
   duplication inventory listed as copied: role determination (self-routed
   vs. registry-guest-mounted; host vs. guest), the split-view flex
   container + `--split-reader-width` custom property + its own testid
   contract, the SplitDivider (width state, restore-on-open, the SetDivider
   commit), hatch invocation, and close plumbing (CloseGuest). Reader.razor's
   and Sources.razor's OWN `_splitOpen`-style branches are RETIRED -- BOTH of
   them (S-2) -- replaced by this ONE shared component, used by both.

   Controller ruling 2: "no separate CanHost flag" -- there is deliberately
   no `CanHost` capability anywhere in this codebase. The declared
   enter-split HATCH (EnterSplitHatch.HostView) IS the hosting declaration --
   one vocabulary, not two. The tripwire against a future "pasted wrapper"
   host is client.Tests' own hatch-conformance test: every hatch's own
   HostView must be found using this component, by name
   (`<CompositionSplit HostName="@ViewNames.X"`), in that view's own source --
   a fourth host with no CompositionSplit usage fails that test, loudly.

   Controller ruling 3 (S-1, CRITICAL): every render decision below is driven
   by a REAL `IViewComposition`/`ICompositionLayout` (`Composition`, via
   `ViewRegistry.ComposeFrom`) -- `Layout.Kind`/`Members` are read off the
   COMPILED CONTRACT TYPES, not `ViewArrangement`'s own bare strings
   directly. Unrecognized `Layout.Kind` fails LOUD (a visible toast,
   `data-testid="composition-error"`) -- and, fix round 1 (Q-6): renders
   INSTEAD of the normal split content, not alongside it, now that
   "loud" is cheap to get right while already rewriting this file.

   ============================================================
   FIX ROUND 2 (re-review "GAPS REMAIN" -- Adjudication F's own unfinished
   tail). Read this section before touching this file OR adding a new host.
   ============================================================

   RULING 3 -- "HOW TO BECOME A HOST," THE OFFICIAL RECIPE (verbatim, per
   controller ruling): after this round, a host that needs NOTHING beyond
   the ordinary case (Sources.razor is the reference example -- read it
   alongside this) carries ONLY:
     1. the `<CompositionSplit HostName="@ViewNames.X">` tag, wrapping its
        own content as `ChildContent` (`Context="ctx"`);
     2. a `[Parameter] public bool? SplitMode { get; set; }` IF its own
        route can be reached as a routed page at all (threaded straight
        into `SplitModeOverride` below) -- a host that is NEVER reached by
        Blazor's own router (impossible for any `@page`-declared component,
        so this applies to every real host so far) still needs this;
     3. a class conditional driven by `ctx` -- as of this round, literally
        just string interpolation against `ctx.HostPaneClassSuffix` (see
        `HostPaneClass` below), never a hand-written
        `ctx.IsSplitOpen &amp;&amp; ctx.IsHost ? "X" : ""` ternary of its own;
     4. a route-level `EnterSingle`/`EnterSplit` dispatch, once, at mount
        (asserting its OWN arrangement -- see Sources.razor's own
        `OnInitializedAsync`).
   THAT IS DECLARATION, NOT DUPLICATED BEHAVIOR -- nothing above re-derives
   role, re-implements close plumbing, or re-subscribes to the arrangement
   atom. A host does NOT need its own `ViewArrangementAtom.Changed`
   subscription, its own `StateHasChanged()` call, or its own
   `IDisposable`/unsubscribe -- see RULING 1 immediately below for why, and
   Reader.razor for the ONE documented exception a host takes on ONLY when
   it genuinely has markup or a side effect this component's own re-render
   cannot reach.

   RULING 1 -- the arrangement subscription lives HERE, exactly once. Fix
   round 1 left the atom's own subscribe/unsubscribe pair duplicated in BOTH
   Reader.razor and Sources.razor (the re-review's own row #12, "not
   consolidated") -- this component now owns the ENTIRE subscribe /
   `StateHasChanged` / unsubscribe trio itself (`OnInitialized`/
   `HandleArrangementChanged`/`Dispose` below). Both host pages DELETED
   their own copies. Since THIS component's own `StateHasChanged()` only
   marks ITS OWN render subtree dirty -- which includes `ChildContent`,
   re-invoked with a freshly computed `ctx` every time -- any host whose
   ENTIRE markup lives inside `ChildContent` (Sources: "declare a hatch,
   nothing else," per the recipe above) needs nothing more; its content
   re-renders for free.

   THE ONE DOCUMENTED EXCEPTION (Reader.razor): a host can have markup OR a
   side effect that lives OUTSIDE `ChildContent`'s own lambda scope (Reader's
   `ExplorerPopover`, a sibling of `<CompositionSplit>`, reads `_compositionSplit`
   directly -- see that `@ref`'s own read sites) or that is a genuine side
   effect tied to the SAME trigger, not a rendering concern at all (Reader's
   `SyncSplitUrl`, a `Nav.NavigateTo` call keeping ITS OWN route's `?split=1`
   truthful -- an arrangement change ELSEWHERE, e.g. World's own hatch or a
   guest's `CloseGuest`, must still resync Reader's URL the next time Reader
   itself is the one routed here). For exactly this case -- and ONLY this
   case -- `OnArrangementChanged` (an optional `Action?` parameter, below) is
   the sanctioned hook: it fires from THIS component's OWN single owned
   subscription, so the trio itself is STILL never duplicated; the host
   supplies one callback, not a second subscribe/unsubscribe pair. This is
   the SAME established shape `OnFractionCommitted` already set (fix round
   1's own "the ONE genuinely per-host behavior") for a second genuinely
   per-host need. A future host that thinks it needs this should double-check
   first: does the need live INSIDE `ChildContent`? If so, it already works
   for free -- do not reach for this hook.

   RULING 2 -- `IsSplitOpen`'s own host-role law (below) now reads
   `Composition.IsHostedBy(HostName)`, an EXTENSION METHOD on the compiled
   `IViewComposition` contract (`client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs`), not an
   inline formula written here. `Reader.razor`'s own `SyncSplitUrl` -- which
   cannot reach `ctx` (it runs from the `OnArrangementChanged` hook above,
   OUTSIDE `ChildContent`'s lambda) -- calls the SAME extension method
   against its own `Registry.ComposeFrom(...)` call, so the "who is host"
   law has exactly ONE definition anywhere in this codebase, never
   re-derived by hand at a second call site (the re-review's own N-2
   finding: Reader.razor:520's pre-fix-round-2 hand copy of this exact
   formula). See `ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs`'s own
   `RoleFormulaRederivation_*` tests for the tripwire against a FUTURE
   hand-copy of this shape.

   RULING 4 -- two hazard classes, now documented where a future host
   author will actually read them (not buried in a 30-line
   `Reader.razor` comment or a batch report's own "concerns" bullet):

   (a) THIS COMPONENT'S OWN `@if`/`else if`/`else` BRANCHES CHANGE
   RENDER-TREE SHAPE, NOT JUST CSS CLASSES. Going from "single" (bare
   `ChildContent`) to "split-h host" (`ChildContent` wrapped in a NEW
   `.split-view` div) is a STRUCTURALLY different render tree -- Blazor's
   diffing algorithm DISCARDS and RECREATES every DOM node inside
   `ChildContent` across that transition (it does not patch classes onto
   the SAME node the way the pre-VC-1 single-div design did). Any JS
   interop that captures a DIRECT DOM ELEMENT REFERENCE inside `ChildContent`
   (e.g. `document.querySelector(...)`, closed over by a `recompute`
   callback) and binds it ONLY on `firstRender` will silently end up bound
   to an ORPHANED node the instant this component's own branch flips --
   this is EXACTLY what happened to `reader.js`'s own `watchChapterNavCenter`
   (fix round 1's own THIRD self-caught live regression, NAV-3/NAV-6: a
   `?split=1` fresh load genuinely renders "single" first, "split-h" second,
   a documented `[SupplyParameterFromQuery]` timing quirk, orphaning a
   `firstRender`-only binding). THE FIX PATTERN: bind on EVERY render
   (guarded only by "is my JS module loaded," never `firstRender`), relying
   on the JS side being IDEMPOTENT and SELF-CLEANING -- unbind/re-query/
   rebind every call, the way `watchChapterNavCenter` already does via its
   own `_navCenterCleanup` (see `reader.js`'s own header). A JS watcher that
   captures a DOM node inside content this component renders and does NOT
   already follow that pattern will break the same way, silently, with no
   compiler or test signal (the re-review's own N-7: there is still no
   automated guard for this class of bug -- `composition.spec.ts`'s own
   `page.on('pageerror')` fixture, added fix round 2, catches a hard CRASH
   from this class, not a silent stale-binding symptom like NAV-3/NAV-6 was).

   (b) NEVER CALL `Nav.NavigateTo` FROM A COMPONENT'S OWN LIFECYCLE
   SYNCHRONOUS PREFIX (the code that runs BEFORE the first genuine `await`
   in `OnInitializedAsync`/`OnParametersSetAsync`) -- not even to the
   CURRENT URL. Fix round 1's own SECOND self-caught live regression: a
   same-URL `Nav.NavigateTo` fired from exactly this window reproduced live
   as `System.InvalidOperationException: InvalidOperation_EnumFailedVersion`
   INSIDE BLAZOR'S OWN `SupplyParameterFromQueryValueProvider.OnLocationChanged`
   -- a framework-internal collection mutated while a location-changed
   notification was still enumerating it. Blazor's navigation pipeline is
   not safe to RE-ENTER from a component's own lifecycle synchronous prefix,
   full stop; a same-URL call is not a safe special case, it still re-enters
   the pipeline. `Reader.razor`'s own `SyncSplitUrl` closes the ONE reachable
   instance of this with a no-op guard (`if (uri == Nav.Uri) return;`) --
   correct today because every reachable path into it happens to yield a
   same-URL no-op, NOT because the guard makes a genuinely different-URL
   call safe from this window. A FUTURE arrangement dispatch reachable from
   a lifecycle synchronous prefix that yields a genuinely DIFFERENT URL
   would reopen this crash with no guard and no red test (re-review's own
   finding, "hazard-class open") -- the real rule is the one stated in this
   sentence, not the narrower guard that happens to satisfy it today. *@
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Contracts
@using BibleAtlas.Client.State
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Views
@implements IDisposable
@inject StateAtom<ViewArrangement> ViewArrangementAtom
@inject ViewRegistry Registry
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@if (!IsGuestMount && !LayoutKinds.IsKnown(Composition.Layout.Kind))
{
    <div class="toast" data-testid="composition-error">
        <p class="toast-message">Unrecognized arrangement layout "@Composition.Layout.Kind" -- ICompositionLayout.Kind is out of vocabulary.</p>
    </div>
}
else if (!IsSplitOpen)
{
    @ChildContent(BuildContext())
}
else if (IsHost)
{
    <div class="split-view" style="--split-reader-width:@(_widthPx.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px;" data-testid="split-view">
        @ChildContent(BuildContext())
        <SplitDivider WidthPx="_widthPx" WidthPxChanged="w => _widthPx = w" OnCommitted="OnDividerCommitted" />
        <div class="split-pane-guest">
            @Registry.Get(Composition.Members[1].Name).Mount(new ViewMountContext(
                SplitMode: true,
                OnRequestClose: EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, RequestCloseAsync),
                RegisterQueryHandler: h => _guestQueryHandler = h))
        </div>
    </div>
}
else
{
    @ChildContent(BuildContext())
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Which registered view is asking. Required: without an
    /// identity, this component cannot tell "I am currently hosting" from
    /// "some OTHER view hosts, I have nothing extra to render here."</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string HostName { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>null = self-determine from the shared atom (the common
    /// case: this view was reached by ordinary Blazor routing, and is
    /// therefore either single or host). Explicit true = this instance is
    /// being mounted as a GUEST via the registry (ViewRegistrySetup's own
    /// mount closures always pass true here, never false -- a
    /// registry-mounted component is, by this batch's own design, ALWAYS a
    /// guest).</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool? SplitModeOverride { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment<CompositionSplitContext> ChildContent { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Optional -- the ONE genuinely per-host behavior Adjudication
    /// F's own inventory named (Reader's own `ViewState.Map.DividerFraction`
    /// persistence; Sources has no such slot and leaves this unbound).
    /// Fired alongside (never instead of) the unconditional `SetDivider`
    /// dispatch below, whenever a real fraction commits.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<double> OnFractionCommitted { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Fix round 2 (Q-1, Important -- "the review's recommended
    /// HostPaneClass parameter was not built"): the class NAME this host's
    /// OWN outer page-root element should carry while it is genuinely the
    /// live split-h host -- e.g. Reader passes "split-pane-reader", Sources
    /// passes "split-pane-host" (see app.css's own header comments on both:
    /// they genuinely differ in body, Reader's own column-width custom
    /// properties vs. the generic fallback shape, so this cannot be a single
    /// wrapper-computed constant). Left null/empty for a host with no such
    /// class (none exist today, but nothing requires one). Combined with the
    /// `IsSplitOpen &amp;&amp; IsHost` condition -- computed HERE, once -- into
    /// <see cref="CompositionSplitContext.HostPaneClassSuffix"/>, so a host
    /// never hand-writes that ternary itself; see this file's own header,
    /// RULING 3's recipe.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? HostPaneClass { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Fix round 2 (ruling 1, N-1): the SANCTIONED hook for a host
    /// with a genuine need beyond what `ChildContent`'s own re-render already
    /// gives it for free -- see this file's own header, "THE ONE DOCUMENTED
    /// EXCEPTION." Fires from this component's OWN single owned
    /// `ViewArrangementAtom.Changed` subscription (<see cref="HandleArrangementChanged"/>
    /// below) -- a host binding this does NOT get a second subscription of
    /// its own; it gets a callback into the ONE this component already
    /// owns. Plain `Action`, not `EventCallback` -- `ViewArrangementAtom.Changed`
    /// is itself a plain, synchronous `event Action?`
    /// (`client/State/StateAtom.cs`), so the honest shape for whatever it
    /// invokes is the same: synchronous, no `Task` to await, no UI-event
    /// rendering integration `EventCallback` exists for. Reader.razor's own
    /// `SyncSplitUrl` is the ONE real binder today.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Action? OnArrangementChanged { get; set; }

    private double _widthPx = 704;
    private bool _dividerRestoreAttempted;
    private IJSObjectReference? _js;
    private Func<string, Task>? _guestQueryHandler;

    private bool IsGuestMount => SplitModeOverride == true;

    // Fix round 2 (N-4, trivia -- re-review, cheap fix, not parked):
    // memoized for the duration of one render pass, keyed on the ATOM
    // VALUE'S OWN REFERENCE (a real, immutable `record` -- the atom only
    // ever swaps in a genuinely NEW instance on a real change, never mutates
    // one in place, so reference equality is exact, not an approximation).
    // Was previously a plain property recomputing (N registry lookups + two
    // LINQ materializations) on EVERY read -- 3-5 times in a single render
    // (the fail-loud condition, IsSplitOpen via BuildContext, the guest
    // Mount call). ComposeFrom itself stays uncached (see its own doc
    // comment) -- this is the one caller-side cache, not a second one.
    private ViewArrangement? _lastArrangement;
    private IViewComposition? _lastComposition;

    private IViewComposition Composition
    {
        get
        {
            if (!ReferenceEquals(_lastArrangement, ViewArrangementAtom.Value))
            {
                // Fix round 2: written as `_lastArrangement =
                // ViewArrangementAtom.Value` (not staged through a local
                // first) DELIBERATELY -- this is the exact shape
                // `ConformanceTests.cs`'s own S-3 no-copy scan
                // (`ArrangementStateAssignmentPattern`) is written to catch,
                // keeping this the ONE sanctioned atom read the scan's own
                // allowlist names, not a second, differently-shaped read
                // that would silently evade it.
                _lastArrangement = ViewArrangementAtom.Value;
                _lastComposition = Registry.ComposeFrom(_lastArrangement);
            }

            return _lastComposition!;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Public -- read via `@ref` by a host page's OWN markup/methods
    /// that sit OUTSIDE this component's own ChildContent lambda scope (e.g.
    /// Reader's ExplorerPopover, its picker-jump handler) so EVERY read site
    /// uses this ONE unambiguous, already-computed value instead of
    /// re-deriving a hand-copied formula (fix round 1, S-4). Fix round 2
    /// (ruling 2, N-2): the host-role half now reads
    /// <see cref="ViewCompositionExtensions.IsHostedBy"/> -- the ONE shared
    /// law, also the exact call Reader.razor's own SyncSplitUrl makes
    /// against its own `Registry.ComposeFrom(...)` where `ctx` is out of
    /// reach -- rather than an inline formula written only here.</summary>
    public bool IsSplitOpen => IsGuestMount || Composition.IsHostedBy(HostName);

    /// <summary>Public, same reasoning as <see cref="IsSplitOpen"/>. Only
    /// meaningful while <see cref="IsSplitOpen"/>.</summary>
    public bool IsHost => !IsGuestMount;

    // Fix round 2 (ruling 1, N-1 -- re-review, binding): the arrangement
    // subscription, OWNED HERE, exactly once -- see this file's own header,
    // RULING 1, for the full reasoning and the one documented exception
    // (OnArrangementChanged above). Both Reader.razor and Sources.razor
    // DELETED their own copies of this trio this round.
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        ViewArrangementAtom.Changed += HandleArrangementChanged;
    }

    private void HandleArrangementChanged()
    {
        StateHasChanged();
        OnArrangementChanged?.Invoke();
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        ViewArrangementAtom.Changed -= HandleArrangementChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>The composed "route a popover-originated scene query into my
    /// currently-mounted guest" delegate -- null while no split-h guest is
    /// mounted, or while the mounted guest never called
    /// <see cref="ViewMountContext.RegisterQueryHandler"/> (i.e. it doesn't
    /// support being a query-routing target at all, e.g. Sources). The host
    /// page reads this via its own `@ref` on this component and forwards it
    /// to its OWN popover's `OnNavigateWorldInSplit` parameter -- the
    /// generalized "no nested-popup rule" (Batch H), unchanged behavior for
    /// reader+world, generically available to any future query-routable
    /// guest.</summary>
    public Func<string, Task> NavigateGuestInSplit => query => _guestQueryHandler?.Invoke(query) ?? Task.CompletedTask;

    private CompositionSplitContext BuildContext() => new(
        IsSplitOpen,
        IsHost,
        EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, InvokeHatchAsync),
        EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, RequestCloseAsync),
        IsSplitOpen && IsHost && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(HostPaneClass) ? $" {HostPaneClass}" : "");

    private Task InvokeHatchAsync() =>
        Registry.Get(HostName).EscapeHatches.First(h => h.Kind == HatchKinds.EnterSplit).Invoke();

    private Task RequestCloseAsync()
    {
        // Correct regardless of who mounted this instance -- CloseGuest's
        // own Apply is "if split-h, single(Members[0])," which needs no
        // knowledge of which guest is asking (client/State/ViewArrangement.cs).
        ViewArrangementAtom.Dispatch(new CloseGuest());
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // Fix round 1 (Adjudication C's own mechanism, relocated here): fires
    // ONCE per completed drag/keyboard-nudge gesture ("commits on drag END
    // only"). _widthPx -- the ACTUAL render source for the divider's pixel
    // position -- is updated unconditionally; the atom (and the optional
    // OnFractionCommitted) only receive the fraction when SplitDivider's own
    // measurement succeeded.
    private async Task OnDividerCommitted((double WidthPx, double? Fraction) result)
    {
        _widthPx = result.WidthPx;
        if (result.Fraction is { } fraction)
        {
            ViewArrangementAtom.Dispatch(new SetDivider(fraction));
            if (OnFractionCommitted.HasDelegate)
            {
                await OnFractionCommitted.InvokeAsync(fraction);
            }
        }
    }

    // Fix round 1 (relocated from Reader.razor's own RestoreDividerWidthFromAtom):
    // restores _widthPx from the atom's own DividerFraction once per
    // split-open transition, via a LIVE measurement of `.split-view`'s own
    // content-box width (the same `getPaneRect` SplitDivider.razor already
    // uses for its own clamp ceiling) -- this component now owns its OWN JS
    // import rather than depending on a host page's cached module reference,
    // since divider-width ownership itself moved here.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (IsSplitOpen && IsHost && !_dividerRestoreAttempted)
        {
            _dividerRestoreAttempted = true;
            if (ViewArrangementAtom.Value.DividerFraction is { } fraction)
            {
                try
                {
                    _js ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/reader.js");
                    var rect = await _js.InvokeAsync<PaneRectDto?>("getPaneRect", ".split-view");
                    if (rect is { Width: > 0 } r)
                    {
                        // Same light sanity floor as the pre-fix-round-1 code
                        // this replaces -- SplitDivider's own next real
                        // drag/nudge re-clamps to its own exact bounds
                        // regardless.
                        _widthPx = Math.Clamp(fraction * r.Width, 200, r.Width - 200);
                        StateHasChanged();
                    }
                }
                catch (Exception)
                {
                    // Fail soft -- keep whatever _widthPx already holds, same
                    // graceful-degrade stance SplitDivider's own
                    // EnsureMaxWidthAsync takes.
                }
            }
        }
        else if (!IsSplitOpen)
        {
            _dividerRestoreAttempted = false;
        }
    }

    // reader.js's own getPaneRect return shape -- a second, independently
    // declared copy of SplitDivider.razor's own private PaneRectDto (that
    // one is file-scoped private too -- same "one shape, many callers, no
    // shared type" precedent that file's own header already establishes).
    private sealed record PaneRectDto(double Left, double Top, double Width, double Height);
}
